From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ram: generate_ram -reset_value, an asynchronous reset
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

-reset_value v resets every word to v asynchronously. Each bit's flop is
cleared or preset by the reset, the other pin held off by one tie cell
per word slice: the data, clear and preset pins are read from the flop's
ff group, and which pin gives which value accounts for the flop's
output polarity (asap7's DFFASRHQNx1 stores !D, so its `clear` sets the
data to 1). The reset port takes the flop's own polarity, rst_n or rst;
a parent adapts any other reset, or a synchronous one, to it. The
storage cell, found or given, has both a clear and a preset. The
behavioral model resets the same way. Not with -use_latch (RAM-0065).

Several ORFS designs reset their register files (ibex, whose ECC and
lockstep checks need valid codewords from the first cycle; cva6,
ariane, swerv, coralnpu); it is an option, off by default.

Tested: src/ram/test/reset_asap7.tcl.

Commit 4 of 23 in OpenROAD #11723, carried here from it. Not
upstreamed; retires at a //:bump onto an OpenROAD that has it.
